      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community was named after Sparta, a city-state in Ancient Greece.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparta%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sparta became the county seat in 1797, incorporated as a town in 1805, and later as a city in 1893.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparta%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1864, during Sherman's March to the Sea, Sparta remained unscathed thanks to Confederate Captain Henry Culver diverting Union troops.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparta%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Sparta hosts Georgia's Hancock State Prison, a notable correctional facility in the region.",
